last time i saw you were in a swanky  hotel in london and here we are  six months later who could possibly have  known what was going to happen  has your life changed in that time oh  it's dramatic that's for sure  had i pitched you a picture of our  future  in this last six months we would have  both laughed it off as a science fiction  movie  and now we're we're having to meet via  zoom  last time we spoke you said uh that you  weren't at a stage to write anything  new certainly as far as an album was  concerned i'm guessing you've had lots  of material over the last six months  yeah hence the two new songs that i'd  added to the record since i did see you  last  now i realized if i was going to write a  topical record and then walk into the  buzz saw of cobot 19 and the george  floyd  death and you know the world kept going  round there was opportunity to  continue my journey on the writing  talk to me about the george floyd song  uh god damn those eight long minutes  um lyric painful song  um and with this whole album  i was witness to history and that's my  position i'm not taking sides i'm just  feel like i'm a reporter and  in retrospect when i came up the album  title 2020 was really meant to be  tongue-in-cheek  and there was a rhinestone a cute bumper  sticker and a t-shirt  but as events started to unfold and i  started to really focus on that  writing one thing led to another and i  said no this is now a snapshot of  of my life and the history that i  witnessed  and so whether i i lived it or read it  or watched it  these are the events that unfolded so  when i  in lockdown saw the  videotape that the rest of the world saw  of the death of george floyd i was moved  maybe it's because we were in lockdown  and couldn't go anywhere else or maybe  the quality of the video  and the audio being right there for all  of us to witness  if that didn't move you i just  questioned your  sense of humanity i wonder if it's made  us  more aware of each other during lockdown  i know you talk about  uh me not we and whether we've changed  our attitude  now when we're more we not just me  what do you think we need to do to look  after each other better  well for instance as it pertains to  mental health issues um don't stigmatize  them  and so as we work in our foundation for  example  with the homeless and those who end up  hungry as a you know as typically a  result of that  when the general public don't take the  time to think  they say well he could just go get a job  or she could just  get off feet well sometimes when mental  illness issues come  into play we have to remember that and  not stigmatizing them is a step in the  right direction  so people don't aspire to to have mental  illness they don't aspire to be homeless  or hungry  and and when we realize that these  things are issues that we can control  uh through health care and assistance  uh i.e foundations that provide  food or shelter we as a people will only  be  better for it how has lockdown changed  for you personally though as a family  man have you found that you've been able  to have more time with your wife  and children yeah like everyone else you  know being forced into lockdown and not  having anywhere to run off to  uh gave you more time with yourself  and so for those who survived it  and were able to still have food on the  table and  shelter it was a reset button and some  good things came out of it opportunities  to get in touch with  those around you or with yourself you  know those who lost their jobs or lost  their health or lost their lives they  were affected in a dramatic way and we  can't forget that  in america we've lost 200 000 people and  counting  it's criminal but um  you know so you have to remember that  too this is serious  what do you put that down to we blame we  blame in part  our government for the way that they um  dealt with certain aspects of um  coronavirus  what do you do in the us well it  you know i don't want to just point  fingers of blame  because it seems too easy and  in fairness the needle was moving  wear a mask don't wear a mask travel  don't travel total lock down don't lock  down  and then it got to a place where we're  politicizing a mask  that's wrong that was just that that  is wrong if a mask was going to stop the  spread  if for nothing else then respect of your  neighbor and your friend  you have a little piece of cloth on your  nose it's not a big deal it's not a  great sacrifice  i think wearing a face mask as i greet  presidents prime ministers dictators  kings queens  i don't know somehow i don't see it for  myself i just  i just don't maybe i'll change my mind  this president  long ago forfeited any moral leadership  in this country  he can't stop the violence because for  years he's formatted it  how do you feel about biden i feel good  i feel good you know he's an experienced  man of the world who as our vice  president and a senator from the state  of delaware  has served the country for probably  close to 50 years now  um i've spoken to him recently  and i found him to be engaging and and  on topic and  and caring um so i feel okay about it  he said you spoke to him recently what  about i had done a fundraiser i'd sung a  couple songs on a zoom  um so you know it was that kind of a  simple engagement you know you see him  on television and and  both candidates are doing their thing  they're preaching their gospel  and the american public's got a tough  decision uh  because the future of the country and  its reflection on the world rely on it  how are you gonna feel um if uh we get  four more years of president trump  that's a nice house you've got there was  there anything  available in the neighborhood i was just  just wandering asking for a friend  you know what's your plan for election  night popcorn and watching the telly too  late  i think you should refer to it and i'll  give you a little tip as election  week it's not gonna be a night  uh i think that's becoming more evident  uh  with every day um so get used to  to being here for a while my prediction  is that you don't call a winner that  night  um but where will i be uh yeah i'll be  glued to the  to the tv and and watching  what happens between now and then you  mean it's going to be too close to call  i just don't think everything will be in  we're going to rely on these mail-in  ballots  five states that have never done it  before uh new jersey  is being one of them is going to rely on  that  as an entertainer do you feel the weight  of responsibility weighing heavy on your  shoulders  we're looking to you to to lift the mood  when we can  finally go out again and potentially go  to a rock concert or wherever  i don't know that it's a responsibility  but every artist has the opportunity to  bear witness to history and to record  pages of the lives that we are  collectively living  um you know when i wrote do what you can  i wrote it simply on an acoustic guitar  and performed it that way  on a little zoom telethon here in new  york and new jersey  but uh i knew how to make it into a  record and the record is big and  optimistic and a  bon jovi kind of an anthem uh and it  brings that sense of optimism  back so what i'm hoping is that in a  year or two from now when i do get to  perform it  people will look back on this moment  remember that coming together  if you can't do what you do you do what  you can and then there will be  kind of that reflective joy looking  back on it you know in our rear view  mirror when you do  your next concert when you do your next  gig you want to do some of  the new stuff which ones will you do you  want to do some of the old stuff  which ones will you do you know the old  ones i'm expected to do  and and i understand that when i'm a fan  of a band i want to hear the hits too  you know the hits are what got us there  but i'm just adding  to that arsenal you know and the new  record i would hope to play as many  songs as i possibly could  i'm as excited about playing the songs  on 2020  as i would be to break out the hits last  time we spoke  um you had just organized to see harry  we are very sad to  lose him here in the united kingdom and  he's been getting a pretty rough ride  any thoughts on that well you know who  are we to comment  until you walk in in his shoes he and i  were actually slated to do something  here in new york  that had been postponed and then  rescheduled and now postponed yet again  so who knows if it it'll ever happen at  this point  but it was around veterans my buddy pete  tanzan you may have heard of him uh he  sends his  regards wow just please even knows who i  am  he certainly does and he's still rocking  he's still looking forward to going on  tour with the who  next year in the united states are you  hoping to still be going  when you're pete's age god bless pete  and roger  you know i wish that all of those guys  and jagger and richards  would quit for one reason at least i  would know where the  end zone was i've been chasing them my  whole career  37 years into it they're not slowing  down and they've been at it for  50 plus years i just want to know where  the end zone is so i can take a nap

